---
import TrainingLayout from "../../layouts/TrainingLayout.astro";
import Snippet from "../../components/training/Snippet.astro";
import LiveDemo from "../../components/training/LiveDemo.astro";
import ApiLink from "../../components/training/ApiLink.astro";
import ChapterLink from "../../components/training/ChapterLink.astro";
import Callout from "../../components/training/Callout.astro";
---
<TrainingLayout slug="inline-editing">

  <h2 id="columns">Make columns editable</h2>
  <p>Editing is switched on per column. Three fields of the <ApiLink name="ColumnDefIf" /> matter:</p>
  <Snippet file="demos/inline-editing.ts" region="editing" title="src/model/order-editing.ts" />
  <ul>
    <li><ApiLink name="ColumnDefIf.editable" label="editable" /> – a function, may depend on the row
      (<code>() =&gt; !locked</code>).</li>
    <li><ApiLink name="ColumnDefIf.editInputPipe" label="editInputPipe" /> – converts the text of the input field before it is
      written into the model: <code>editInputPipeForNumber</code> turns <code>"12"</code> into <code>12</code>.</li>
    <li><ApiLink name="ColumnDefIf.getEditRenderer" label="getEditRenderer" /> – the editor. Default: a text input
      (<ApiLink name="InputCellRenderer" />); here a <ApiLink name="SelectCellRenderer" /> with <ApiLink name="ValueLabel" /> options.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Start editing: double-click a cell, or press <kbd>F2</kbd> on the focused cell. The value is taken over when the editor fires
    its <code>change</code> event – <kbd>Enter</kbd> or leaving the field for a text input, picking an option for a select.</p>

  <h2 id="after">After the edit</h2>
  <p>
    The table writes the new value into your object (via <ApiLink name="AreaModelIf.setValue" />) and reports it:
    <ApiLink name="EventListenerIf.onModelChanged" /> with a <ApiLink name="GeModelChangeEvent" />. Derived values are your job:
  </p>
  <Snippet file="demos/inline-editing.ts" region="events" />
  <Snippet file="demos/inline-editing.ts" region="wire" />
  <LiveDemo name="inline-editing" title="Edit quantity and status" height={400}
            caption="Double-click a quantity, type 20, press Enter: total and footer update. Double-click a status: a select box." />

  <Callout type="tip" title="The row stays where it is">
    <p>After an edit the row is not moved, even if the table is sorted by that column – users would lose it. Re-sort explicitly
      with <ApiLink name="TableApi.reSort" /> when it makes sense (e.g. on a button).</p>
  </Callout>
  <Callout type="version">
    <p>Needs <code>@guiexpert/table@1.1.89</code>: the editor now opens on cells with renderers (bars, badges, components), only in
      the body, keeps its focus, and shortcuts like ⌘/Ctrl+A no longer destroy your input.</p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="saving">Saving</h2>
  <p>
    The table has no notion of a backend. Send the change from <code>onModelChanged</code> – the event contains row and column,
    the new value is already in your object – and, if the server rejects it, write the old value back and repaint.
  </p>

  <h2 id="fill-batch">Fill handle and batch edit</h2>
  <Callout type="version">
    <p>Fill handle and batch edit are new in 1.1.96.</p>
  </Callout>
  <p>
    Users who edit a lot want spreadsheet comfort: select cells, drag the small square at the bottom right corner – one value
    is copied, two numbers continue as a series, dates count up, <code>Item 9</code> becomes <code>Item 10</code>. And they
    want to look at their changes before anything is saved. Two options switch both on:
    <ApiLink name="TableOptionsIf.fillHandle" label="fillHandle" /> and <ApiLink name="TableOptionsIf.batchEdit" label="batchEdit" />.
  </p>
  <Snippet file="demos/fill-batch.ts" region="options" title="src/main.ts" />
  <p>
    Only editable cells are filled, and every value runs through <code>editInputPipe</code> and the new
    <ApiLink name="ColumnDefIf.validate" label="validate" /> – exactly like a typed value:
  </p>
  <Snippet file="demos/fill-batch.ts" region="columns" title="src/model/order-columns.ts" />
  <p>
    With batch edit the cells show the new values (marked yellow, invalid ones red) while your <code>orders</code> stay as
    they are. <ApiLink name="EventListenerIf.onBatchEditChanged" label="onBatchEditChanged" /> tells you how many changes
    are pending – the moment to enable a "Save" button. <ApiLink name="TableApi.getPendingChanges" /> lists them with row,
    property, old and new value for your server; <ApiLink name="TableApi.commitBatchEdit" /> writes them (one
    <code>onModelChanged</code> with all cells), <ApiLink name="TableApi.cancelBatchEdit" /> drops them:
  </p>
  <Snippet file="demos/fill-batch.ts" region="events" title="src/order-events.ts" />
  <Snippet file="demos/fill-batch.ts" region="save" title="src/main.ts" />
  <LiveDemo name="fill-batch" title="Fill handle and batch edit" height={400}
            caption="Drag the small square below the two selected quantities. Double-click a quantity and type 0: the cell turns red and save refuses." />
  <Callout type="tip" title="Keyboard and screen readers">
    <p><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>/<kbd>⌘</kbd>+<kbd>D</kbd> copies the top row of the selection down, <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>/<kbd>⌘</kbd>+<kbd>R</kbd>
      the left column to the right – only while the fill handle is on and a range is selected, otherwise the browser keeps its
      keys. Screen readers hear "3 cells filled" and "2 changes not saved". Sorting and filtering keep pending changes on
      their rows; totals, copy and Excel use the saved data. All options: instructions.md 6.17.</p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="next">Next</h2>
  <p>Not the user changes the values, the market does: <ChapterLink slug="live-data" />.</p>
</TrainingLayout>
